----------------------------------------------------------------
-- The check is per relation.  Another table's TRUNCATE replayed while a
-- cursor is mid-scan leaves the cursor alone, every toast value it fetches
-- included; the truncated table itself is refused in the same transaction
-- and at the next statement, until a newer anchor is published.  A RENAME
-- after the anchor updates the pg_class row but keeps the file: the update
-- chain is walked and the read goes on.  A relation created after the
-- anchor did not exist at it, nor did an index created after it, which the
-- planner opens whether or not it uses it (ORCA opens an index only in a
-- plan that uses it, so that statement runs with the planner, and the
-- planner refuses every statement on the table until the next publication).
-- A TRUNCATE in another database touches nothing here.
----------------------------------------------------------------
